WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You were here in June 2017.
-Yeah.

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:04.838 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And I remember spending
the whole day

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:06.773 size:84% position:42%
making sure I could say
your last name right.

00:00:06.773 --> 00:00:10.744
And now you're so well-known,
it just rolls off my tongue.

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:12.145 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Are you just as surprised

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:15.248 align:start size:94% position:6%
at the reaction you've gotten
in these early days

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:17.283 align:start size:94% position:6%
of sort of
announcing your candidacy?

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:18.084 line:92%
-Yeah, just the speed of it.

00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:20.820
I mean, obviously, we were
hoping for a good reaction.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.222 align:start size:94% position:6%
We were hoping
for a good response.

00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:24.090 align:start size:94% position:6%
But the way this thing
has taken off

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
has passed even our most
optimistic scenarios.

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:28.528 align:start size:94% position:6%
So now we've got a lot of work
to do to make sure we have

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:29.829 align:start size:94% position:6%
the organizing
and the substance behind it

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:32.365
to really take us all the way,
but it's a fantastic beginning.

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:36.569
-In the early days, before you
had name and face recognition,

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:37.604 line:92%
were there humbling moments

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:38.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you were out
on the campaign trail?

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:40.573 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, yeah.
I mean, not that long ago,

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:43.109
I was getting ready to board
a flight to New Hampshire.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:45.678 align:start size:94% position:6%
A few people recognized me,
came up, said hello.

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:47.547
And then, the lady
standing right next to me said,

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:49.049 align:start size:94% position:6%
"You must be Pete."
And I was like, "Yeah."

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:50.950 align:start size:94% position:6%
She clearly
had seen these interactions.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:51.785 line:92%
And we had a nice little chat.

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:52.952 size:88% position:44%
She said, "Why are you
going to New Hampshire?"

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:54.454
I said, "Well, 'cause of
the campaign, of course."

00:00:54.454 --> 00:00:55.588 align:start size:94% position:6%
She said,
"Oh, right, right, right.

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:58.291 align:start size:94% position:6%
What station
are you a reporter for?"

00:00:58.291 --> 00:00:59.125 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:59.125 --> 00:01:01.828 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was one of those moments
that cut me back down to size.

00:01:01.828 --> 00:01:02.262 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:01:02.262 --> 00:01:04.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I forgot that not everybody
knows who I am.

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:06.466
But, you know, that's the thing
you gotta remember is,

00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:08.568 align:start size:94% position:6%
you can feel like --
You can get into this bubble

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:10.570 align:start size:94% position:6%
and feel like everybody's
heard your message

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:11.604 line:92%
when actually a lot of people

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:13.406 size:84% position:42%
still haven't dialed in
for the process yet.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:14.841 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're not following
the blow-by-blow.

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:17.710
And my job is to find as many
different ways as possible

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.579 align:start size:94% position:6%
to get the message out
to new people.

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:22.115 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I want to ask real quick,
before I get back

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:24.184 align:start size:94% position:6%
to your campaign
and your candidacy,

00:01:24.184 --> 00:01:26.953 align:start size:94% position:6%
what was your initial reaction
to what we saw today

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:29.222
from Barr and the release
of the Mueller report?

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:31.658 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I mean, I guess it confirmed
a lot of things

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:32.459 line:92%
that we were worried about,

00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:34.694
that we have a president
who acts in a self-serving way,

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:39.966
that there was a lot of behavior
that was, at best, unethical

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:42.502 align:start size:94% position:6%
and legally problematic,
to put it charitably.

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:44.337 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know, the way that
the Attorney General

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:47.140
conducted himself, as though
he were the personal attorney

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.943 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the President,
was incredibly troubling, too.

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:51.644 align:start size:94% position:6%
At the same time,
I think, politically,

00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:52.946 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm not sure
it will change much.

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:55.548 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's one more reminder
that if we really want

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:59.185 align:start size:94% position:6%
to send Trumpism
into the history books,

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:02.689 size:88% position:44%
the best thing we can do
is defeat it decisively

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:05.225 align:start size:94% position:6%
at the ballot box in 2020.
-You have --

00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:14.334 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:15.869 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
They're, uh...

00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:17.303 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Cheers and applause
continues ]

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:20.406 size:84% position:42% line:92%
I'm very good at, uh...

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:23.443
I'm very good at judging how the
audience feels about something.

00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:25.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're split
right down the middle on this.

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.413 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:30.049 line:92%
-You have a military background.

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
One of the other things
that's been true, I think,

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:35.822 size:81% position:41%
for the last two years
but especially sort of

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:38.358 align:start size:94% position:6%
upholding the focus today
is that Donald Trump

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:41.895
sort of always seems to want
to dismiss military intelligence

00:02:41.895 --> 00:02:44.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
while at the same time
boasting about

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:45.698 size:81% position:41%
what a great supporter
of the military he is.

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:49.836 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is that a hypocrisy that is --
do you feel more acutely

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:51.271
felt by you because of
your military background?

00:02:51.271 --> 00:02:54.240
-Yeah, I mean, the thing that's
upsetting is when you're --

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.308 align:start size:94% position:6%
especially
in military intelligence,

00:02:55.308 --> 00:02:58.478
when you're deployed,
like when I was in Afghanistan,

00:02:58.478 --> 00:02:59.179 size:88% position:44% line:92%
you do work believing --

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:02.081
You know, the highest honor,
really, that could happen to you

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:04.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
as a member of
the intelligence community

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:07.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that your work
goes to a policymaker

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:08.221 line:92%
to help them make a decision.

00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:10.223 align:start size:94% position:6%
You believe that they read
these things carefully.

00:03:10.223 --> 00:03:12.559 align:start size:94% position:6%
And when people are putting
their lives on the line

00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:14.894 align:start size:94% position:6%
in order to support
U.S. security

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:16.863 align:start size:94% position:6%
through good intelligence,
you know,

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:19.299 align:start size:94% position:6%
that whole system
rests on the belief

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:21.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the president,
the generals,

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:25.038
that they're using that infor--
or at least reading it, right?

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:26.839 align:start size:94% position:6%
But that they are
using it wisely.

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:29.342
To see the way that intelligence
is being politicized,

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:33.813
to see the way that military
families are being treated

00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:36.849 align:start size:94% position:6%
as props by this president
is demoralizing.

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:38.885 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's one more reason
why it might not be a bad idea

00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:42.155
to have somebody in the
White House who actually served.

00:03:42.155 --> 00:03:44.991 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:46.326 line:92%
I think there's this question,

00:03:46.326 --> 00:03:49.162 line:92%
and, obviously, in order to be

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:50.496 align:start size:94% position:6%
a successful
Democratic candidate,

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:52.165 align:start size:94% position:6%
you have
to bring together people

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:54.300 line:92%
from many diverse backgrounds.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.603 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's obviously
a more diverse voting base

00:03:56.603 --> 00:03:57.437 line:92%
than I think what you think of

00:03:57.437 --> 00:04:00.139
as sort of a historically
Republican voting base.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:03.443 size:84% position:42%
Yet you -- So, when you
make a decision, like,

00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:05.345 align:start size:94% position:6%
do you just commit
to the Democratic base?

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:09.782 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or when you make
an approach to Trump voters,

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:11.117 line:92%
how do you speak to them?

00:04:11.117 --> 00:04:14.821 align:start size:94% position:6%
What message is it that you,
or any of your colleagues

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:17.857 align:start size:94% position:6%
who are trying to get
this Democratic nomination,

00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:18.691 size:81% position:41% line:92%
approach Trump voters?

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:19.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
What's the message
you try to give?

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:20.827 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, look,
there are some voters

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:21.928 align:start size:94% position:6%
who we're never gonna
get through to.

00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:23.329 align:start size:94% position:6%
But there are many others
that I think we can.

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:25.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
If somebody voted
repeatedly Democratic

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:27.000 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then voted
for this President,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
out of any number of reasons,
but basically voted

00:04:30.036 --> 00:04:30.803 size:84% position:42% line:92%
to burn the house down,

00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:33.606 align:start size:94% position:6%
I want that voter
back voting Democrat again.

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:35.942 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they're --
-'Cause they're sitting there

00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:38.211
in a house that burned down,
and they're like, "Oh, no."

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:39.345 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:41.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-"This was a terrible idea."

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:42.915 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:43.716 line:92%
-Well, you know, to some extent,

00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:46.586 align:start size:94% position:6%
it came from a level of
frustration around the system.

00:04:46.586 --> 00:04:49.122
And that's not to excuse
the racism and other things

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:49.956 line:92%
that went into that campaign.

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:53.926
But I think people are more
susceptible to those messages

00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:56.095 align:start size:94% position:6%
of racism and xenophobia
if they also feel

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:59.132 align:start size:94% position:6%
a certain level
of insecurity or uncertainty

00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:01.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
in their economic
and social lives.

00:05:01.601 --> 00:05:03.336 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so what I think
we've got to do is,

00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:04.370 align:start size:94% position:6%
we've got to reach out
to those voters,

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:07.240
reminding them that we're the
ones trying to get you a raise,

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.008 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're the ones trying to get
paid family leave,

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:11.344 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're the ones trying to stop
the Republican Congress

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:12.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
from taking away
your healthcare.

00:05:12.178 --> 00:05:14.881
And while we do that --
and this is the important

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:19.986
thing alongside that -- we never
budge on our bedrock commitment

00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:20.820 line:92%
to racial and social justice.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.022 align:start size:94% position:6%
'Cause that's where
our moral authority as a party

00:05:23.022 --> 00:05:25.391 line:92%
comes from in the first place.

